common: block in the synchronous wait instead of spinning

ssc_common_wait_sync_context() iterated the default main context with
may_block FALSE, so every synchronous call (ssc_sensor_*_new_sync,
_open_sync, _close_sync) burned a full core until its callback ran.
iio-sensor-proxy, which calls open_sync and close_sync on every first
claim and last release of a sensor, used 92 % of a core for minutes
when an open did not complete.

Iterate with may_block TRUE. The completion callback is dispatched on
the same context, so the loop still wakes for it, and the thread sleeps
between events.
---
 src/libssc-common.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/libssc-common.c b/src/libssc-common.c
index 3648f47..0569336 100644
--- a/src/libssc-common.c
+++ b/src/libssc-common.c
@@ -53,7 +53,7 @@ ssc_common_wait_sync_context (SyncContext *ctx) {
 	g_mutex_lock (&ctx->mutex);
 	while (!ctx->finished) {
 		g_mutex_unlock (&ctx->mutex);
-		g_main_context_iteration (g_main_context_default (), FALSE);
+		g_main_context_iteration (g_main_context_default (), TRUE);
 		g_mutex_lock (&ctx->mutex);
 	}
 	g_mutex_unlock (&ctx->mutex);
